# TB-X304F LK2nd reboot-mode evidence

Recorded in samcday/pocketboot commit
7fe558abb4e00fef84f381033c0783338d1e9436; later consolidated at
a7d0397cc3e01e4fe2bb1bb9aa667bc88fcc8006.
This concerns LK2nd's reboot-bootloader path, not Linux panic restart.

## Device and build

Lenovo TB-X304F / APQ8017, QRD SKU5, NT35521S video panel.
Stock fastboot was unlocked; secure=yes, critical-unlocked=false.
LK2nd source: msm8916-mainline/lk2nd
517bb38a409d4ac982f09e83a046e4dc71be5029, target lk2nd-msm8952.
The APQ8017 QRD-SKU5 match addition was present in the tested images.
This has not been tested on the original author's unit or Xiaomi ferrari.

## Observations

The default MSM8952 PON/hard-reset build accepted a serial-selected
fastboot reboot bootloader but the tablet returned to Android USB
17ef:7bc7 without ADB, not stock fastboot.

The lab override below retained USE_PON_REBOOT_REG=1 by default and allowed
an explicit USE_PON_REBOOT_REG=0 build, selecting LK's existing IMEM cookie
and warm-reset implementation. The build invocation was:

    make -j16 TOOLCHAIN_PREFIX=arm-none-eabi- \
        LK2ND_DTBS=msm8917-qrd-sku5.dtb \
        LK2ND_FORCE_FASTBOOT=1 DEBUG_FBCON=0 USE_PON_REBOOT_REG=0 \
        LK2ND_VERSION=517bb38-tbx304f-lab-imem lk2nd-msm8952

Image: 299024 bytes, SHA-256
132ce182d877b42c1645c84f6108202039736b253b9d543929ca257ef2f98d1d.
A controlled reboot-bootloader test from that running LK2nd image returned
to verified stock product=MSM8917 in 2.08 seconds; the image RAM-booted again.
The no-override and explicit-zero generated build settings were checked.
No replacement bootloader was flashed. These results support investigating
the protocol selection but are not a controlled proof of every reset-mode
interaction or a claim about other MSM8952 boards.

## Source rationale and limits

The Lenovo downstream reset path writes bootloader cookie 0x77665500 to IMEM;
the board removes qcom,store-hard-reset-reason, pointing at a warm-reset
path rather than a retained PON hard-reset reason. Sources inspected:
https://github.com/lenovo-devs/android_kernel_lenovo_msm8953/blob/lineage-16.0-tbx304/drivers/power/reset/msm-poweroff.c
https://github.com/lenovo-devs/android_kernel_lenovo_msm8953/blob/lineage-16.0-tbx304/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/tbx304/tbx304-msm-pm8937.dtsi

This does not establish stock aboot's complete mode-selection logic.
Linux emergency restart uses a different path: panic=-1 alone later returned
to Android while stock boot was intact. An owner-authorized erase of only
boot subsequently supplied a fastboot fallback. That later workaround must
not be presented as LK2nd reboot-mode validation.

Follow-up belongs in samcday/lk2nd: reproduce both protocols from a fixed
build, determine a suitable board-scoped selection, preserve other boards'
default behavior, and test reboot/reboot-bootloader/recovery separately.
PocketBoot should not carry an LK2nd patch stack for this investigation.

## Archived prototype (not an upstream-ready fix)

~~~~diff
Subject: [PATCH] project: msm8952: allow selecting the IMEM restart protocol

Bootstrap experiment for lk2nd 517bb38a409d4ac982f09e83a046e4dc71be5029.
Keep the existing PON default for other builds. The TB-X304F lab build can
select USE_PON_REBOOT_REG=0 to use LK's existing IMEM cookie and warm-reset
path for bootloader/recovery requests.

The Lenovo TB-X304 kernel writes 0x77665500 to IMEM for "bootloader" and
deletes qcom,store-hard-reset-reason from the board's PON node. In contrast,
the default LK2nd PON/hard-reset path returned this unit to Android when
asked to reboot to its bootloader. This override follows that source
evidence. The TB-X304F lab build with value 0 subsequently returned to stock
fastboot in 2.08 seconds in a controlled reboot-bootloader test.

diff --git a/project/msm8952.mk b/project/msm8952.mk
--- a/project/msm8952.mk
+++ b/project/msm8952.mk
@@ -137,7 +137,8 @@
 
 #Use PON register for reboot reason
 ENABLE_REBOOT_MODULE := 1
-DEFINES += USE_PON_REBOOT_REG=1
+USE_PON_REBOOT_REG ?= 1
+DEFINES += USE_PON_REBOOT_REG=$(USE_PON_REBOOT_REG)
 
 # Enable unit test FW
 ifeq ($(ENABLE_UNITTEST_FW),1)
~~~~
